\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Convention}
\title{What intelligence means in this note}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}

\p{We use \strong{task capability} for the model's measured ability to
produce successful behaviour under a declared evaluation procedure. A
capability statement must name the task distribution, the success rule, and
the inference budget. Intelligence is informal shorthand for a collection
of such capabilities.}

\p{This is an operational convention adopted for FTIP. It makes no claim
about consciousness, general agency, or a single latent factor shared by all
tasks. It also keeps capability separate from the cost of exhibiting it: two
policies may reach the same success rate with different numbers of samples,
tokens, or environment interactions.}

\p{The convention follows the measurement practice of reinforcement learning
and language-model evaluation, where a policy is assessed by outcomes under a
specified protocol; see the overview in \citef{murphy2024reinforcement}. Later
trees give these ingredients mathematical types.}
